1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,020
The next section of your text deals with blistering dermatoses,

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:05,860
and we need to focus here on the word blister.

3
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:11,100
Remember that a blister is sort of like a space or a bubble within the skin,

4
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:15,640
and it arises when you have separation of layers of the skin.

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:18,120
So if this is the epidermis and this is the dermis,

6
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:21,140
imagine if the basal cells, which are down here,

7
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:24,000
separated from the basement membrane of the dermis,

8
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:28,020
then what would happen is you would get this big blister within the skin

9
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:30,540
or this bubble-like space that would be full of fluid.

10
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:36,780
Additionally, let's imagine that you've got separation of the different layers of the epidermis.

11
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:39,780
So imagine if this layer here is separated from the rest.

12
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:45,040
That would also produce a bubble in the skin or a vesicle in the skin full of fluid,

13
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:47,120
and again, that would also be called a blister.

14
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:50,460
So the idea here is that we're going to separate layers of the skin,

15
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:55,300
and then that's going to produce a bubble-like vesicle within the skin or a blister.

16
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:57,860
The first disorder is pemphigus vulgaris.

17
00:00:58,180 --> 00:01:01,760
This is when you get autoimmune destruction of the desmosomes,

18
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:08,180
and it's due to an IgG antibody against a component of the desmosomes called desmoglion.

19
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:13,900
Now let's just go back and remind ourselves that if this is the dermal-epidermal junction,

20
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:16,120
so epidermis here and dermis here,

21
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:17,840
you've got your basal cells,

22
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,780
which are the keratinocytes that are up against the basement membrane,

23
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:25,140
and then remember that the next layer is called the stratum spinosum or the spinal layer.

24
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:28,360
These cells are connected by desmosomes.

25
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:34,540
Now if you've got an IgG antibody against desmoglion that's going to trash these desmosomes,

26
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:36,480
when you trash these desmosomes,

27
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:43,420
what's going to happen is that you're going to create a big vesicle in the skin here

28
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:48,520
in between the basal cells and the rest of the epidermis.

29
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:51,580
So you'd have additional epidermis up here, for example.

30
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:55,300
And so that is the classic finding in pemphigus vulgaris.

31
00:01:55,380 --> 00:01:58,980
Now look at this very carefully because it'll take you very far.

32
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:01,160
Notice that the basal cells will be intact,

33
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:03,080
so the basal layer here will be fine.

34
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:06,220
And then on top of that, you'll have the rest of the epidermis,

35
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:09,680
and in between, you'll have this space or this blister,

36
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:13,080
which again is the hallmark of pemphigus vulgaris.

37
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:15,500
Now let's look at this on histology.

38
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:17,000
So here's the dermis.

39
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:18,780
These are the basal cells.

40
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:20,600
Here is the blister.

41
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:26,460
And then here is the remainder of the epidermis that's been separated from the basal layer.

42
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:27,840
Why has it been separated?

43
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:31,480
Because you have an IgG antibody against desmoglion,

44
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:33,520
which is a component of the desmosomes.

45
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,880
And so you've separated some of the cells that were connected by desmosomes.

46
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:41,140
Now a few terms just to be aware of looking at this picture.

47
00:02:41,540 --> 00:02:44,040
One thing that's going to happen is you get acantholysis.

48
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:47,020
And acantholysis is when you separate keratinocytes.

49
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:50,820
So of course, we have acantholysis of the stratum spinosum,

50
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:52,980
which then results in this blister.

51
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:55,960
Remember that the basal cells are going to remain attached.

52
00:02:55,960 --> 00:03:02,220
And the reason why the basal cells remain attached is because the basal cells are attached to the basement membrane

53
00:03:02,220 --> 00:03:05,240
via hemidesmosomes instead of desmosomes.

54
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:08,260
And so this is also called the tombstone appearance

55
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:13,340
because it appears that you have these little tombs sitting here against the basement membrane.

56
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:20,220
Now another important consequence clinically from this image is that this epidermis is relatively thin.

57
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:22,440
Notice that it doesn't contain all of the layers.

58
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:24,320
It just contains some of the layers.

59
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:30,380
And so the wall, which is up here, the wall of this blister, will actually be very thin-walled, quote-unquote.

60
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:33,320
So the bullae, or the blisters, will rupture easily.

61
00:03:33,740 --> 00:03:38,700
And when these rupture, you'll get these shallow erosions with dried crust on the surface of the skin.

62
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:42,980
An additional important finding is that when you have a patient with Pumphagus bulgaris,

63
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:46,380
you can do a biopsy, and immunofluorescence can be performed.

64
00:03:46,380 --> 00:03:52,280
And when immunofluorescence is performed, it will show IgG antibody surrounding the keratinocytes.

65
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:56,700
And they call that the fishnet appearance of immunofluorescence.

66
00:03:57,160 --> 00:04:00,180
One of the hallmarks of Pumphagus bulgaris, and particularly high yield,

67
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:04,000
is that it can involve both the skin and the oral mucosa.

68
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,860
So it's very important to note that Pumphagus bulgaris can also involve the oral mucosa.

69
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:12,540
The next disorder is bullous pemphigoid.

70
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:16,100
This is when you get an autoimmune destruction of the hemidesmosomes.

71
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:18,120
Let's go back and remind ourselves.

72
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:20,300
This is the epidermis.

73
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:21,380
This is the dermis.

74
00:04:21,740 --> 00:04:28,180
Recall that the basal cells are connected to the basement membrane via hemidesmosomes.

75
00:04:28,180 --> 00:04:33,820
If you destroy these hemidesmosomes, the entire epidermis will be lifted off the dermis,

76
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:39,440
and you'll get a blister in between the epidermis and the dermis.

77
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:42,640
And so this is the basic understanding of bullous pemphigoid.

78
00:04:42,820 --> 00:04:44,500
Now let's go back and read your text.

79
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:47,660
You get autoimmune destruction of the hemidesmosomes.

80
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:51,340
It's due to an IgG antibody against the basement membrane.

81
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:55,580
Now because the IgG antibody is going to line up against the basement membrane,

82
00:04:55,580 --> 00:04:58,040
that's going to give you a linear immunofluorescence.

83
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:01,840
It presents a subepidermal blisters of the skin.

84
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:03,240
Why subepidermal?

85
00:05:03,400 --> 00:05:06,180
Because you've lifted off the epidermis from the dermis,

86
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:09,140
and so the blisters will be underneath the epidermis,

87
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:11,400
and the oral mucosa would be spared.

88
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:16,180
And that's important because clinically it helps to distinguish the disease from pemphigus bulgaris,

89
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:18,000
which normally involves the oral mucosa.

90
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:23,920
An additional important clinical finding will be that you'll have tense bullae that do not rupture easily.

91
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:26,000
Now why do these blisters not rupture easily?

92
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:27,480
Because the wall is thick.

93
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:29,640
It contains the entire epidermis.

94
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:32,880
And this can be contrasted against pemphigus bulgaris,

95
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:37,180
because remember that in pemphigus bulgaris you had bullae that ruptured easily

96
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:39,460
because the wall was much thinner,

97
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:41,980
and that was because it was only part of the epidermis.

98
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:45,940
Here's a classic histology just to remind you of some of the principles.

99
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:49,860
So here's the epidermis, here's the dermis, here's the basal cells,

100
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:58,960
and you can clearly see here that you've got separation of the epidermis from the dermis producing a blister.

101
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:04,460
Now of course you've lifted off the basal cells, so there are no basal cells present here.

102
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:09,980
This results in a thick wall, which clinically does not rupture as easily.

103
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:17,060
And in addition, if you did immunofluorescence, you would see antibody lining up against the basement membrane in a linear pattern.

104
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:20,300
So these are all some of the key hallmarks of bullous pemphigoid.

105
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:25,920
Here's a clinical picture of bullous pemphigoid, and you can see that many of these vesicles have not ruptured,

106
00:06:26,020 --> 00:06:28,740
again, due to the fact that they have a thick wall.

107
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:36,700
Dermatitis herpetiformis is when you get autoimmune deposition of IgA at the tips of the dermal papillae.

108
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:38,740
Let's go back and remind ourselves.

109
00:06:39,560 --> 00:06:44,980
Here is the epidermis, here is the dermis, these are the dermal papillae,

110
00:06:44,980 --> 00:06:50,140
and remember that within the dermal papillae you've got these blood vessels which help to feed the epidermis.

111
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:55,560
Now what's going to happen here is you're going to get deposition just at the tip of the dermal papillae,

112
00:06:55,620 --> 00:06:57,360
for example, here and here.

113
00:06:57,740 --> 00:07:03,520
Now when you get this deposition, that's going to result in the tiny blister at the dermal papillae.

114
00:07:03,860 --> 00:07:08,060
And these tiny blisters, they resemble the tiny vesicles that you see in herpes.

115
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:12,200
And so hence the name dermatitis herpetiformis.

116
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:16,800
It's forming little blisters that mimic the vesicles that you would see with herpes.

117
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:22,060
Now again, this is going to present as pruritic vesicles that are grouped together.

118
00:07:22,060 --> 00:07:25,460
And finally, there's a strong association with celiac disease.

119
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:26,900
This is very high yield.

120
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:30,900
The IgA antibody that was depositing at the tip of the dermal papillae,

121
00:07:30,900 --> 00:07:38,120
these are antibodies against gluten that cross-react with reticulant fibers that attach the basement membrane to the dermis.

122
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:42,840
And that's particularly high yield because if you treat the celiac disease with dietary control

123
00:07:42,840 --> 00:07:48,260
in the same way that the celiac will resolve, the dermatitis herpetiformis will also resolve.

124
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:54,880
Here's a picture of dermatitis herpetiformis just to show you that you've got these grouped tiny little vesicles,

125
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:57,140
which are herpes-like lesions.

126
00:07:57,600 --> 00:08:00,260
Again, this will resolve with a gluten-free diet.

127
00:08:00,260 --> 00:08:04,220
The final disorder in this section is erythema multiforme.

128
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:10,480
This is when you get a hypersensitivity reaction, which leads to the formation of a targetoid rash with bullae.

129
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:16,100
It most commonly is associated with HSV infection, but it can also be seen with mycoplasma,

130
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:18,440
with some drugs such as penicillin.

131
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:21,080
It can be seen as a consequence of autoimmune disease.

132
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:24,280
For example, lupus patients can develop erythema multiforme.

133
00:08:24,280 --> 00:08:27,120
And it can also be seen in association with malignancy.

134
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:31,240
But again, the most common association is HSV infection.

135
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:34,200
Here's a clinical image of erythema multiforme.

136
00:08:34,460 --> 00:08:39,140
Note that you have a bunch of these erythematous rashes, hence the term erythema.

137
00:08:39,460 --> 00:08:42,000
And notice that they're multiple, hence the term multiforme.

138
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:47,000
If you look carefully, you can see that some of these lesions have a little bit of white in their center.

139
00:08:47,420 --> 00:08:54,060
And the reason that they have white in their center is because there is necrosis going on in the very center of that lesion.

140
00:08:54,060 --> 00:08:57,300
And that's what gives it what we call the targetoid appearance.

141
00:08:58,100 --> 00:09:00,720
Erythema multiforme normally involves the skin.

142
00:09:00,940 --> 00:09:07,100
However, if there's oral mucosa involvement with fever, then we call that whole syndrome Stephen-Johnson syndrome.

143
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:13,660
And then there's a super severe form of Stephen-Johnson syndrome, which is called toxic epidermal necrolysis.

144
00:09:14,160 --> 00:09:19,360
And in TEN, you get diffuse sloughing of the skin, which sort of resembles a large burn.

145
00:09:19,820 --> 00:09:23,500
And TEN most often arises due to an adverse drug reaction.

146
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:25,860
Of course, TEN is a medical emergency.

147
00:09:26,540 --> 00:09:28,360
And that concludes this particular section.

